 2. 💪 Dems&#x27; new heartland muscle 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: AP, Axios analysis. Chart: Andrew Pantazi/Axios Democrats' midterm gains extended deep into the machinery of state government: They swept every statewide executive office on the ballot in Texas, Georgia, Nevada, Wisconsin and Arizona, Axios' Andrew Pantazi reports. 

 Why it matters: These low-profile victories will shape what millions of Americans pay for electricity, what their kids read in class and which party runs the 2028 presidential election in key states. 🖼️ The big picture: The Democratic gains reach offices that decide which policies advance, which don't and which get challenged in court.

 Attorneys general: Nathan Johnson in Texas and Tanya Miller in Georgia will decide when their states sue the Trump administration and what corporations they investigate. Secretaries of state: Democrats flipped these offices in Georgia, Ohio and Indiana, putting new officials in charge of running the 2028 presidential election there. Texas' lieutenant governor: Vikki Goodwin will preside over the Senate, where the lieutenant governor picks committee chairs and decides which bills get a hearing. Between the lines: Some offices are hyper-specific and rarely command national attention, but they have a big influence on the issues Democrats championed this election.

 💡 Utility bills: Shelia Edwards' victory and Peter Hubbard's reelection give Democrats a 3–2 majority on Georgia's Public Service Commission, which sets utility rates and approves long-term electricity plans, putting Democrats in charge of decisions that affect the climate and affordability. ⛽ Oil and gas: Jon Rosenthal's victory breaks Republicans' monopoly on Texas' three-member Railroad Commission, which, despite its name, regulates oil and gas production and pipeline safety . 🎒 Classrooms: Democrats won three Republican-held Texas State Board of Education seats and lead in a fourth that would change the majority. Publishers have historically tailored the nation's textbooks to the Texas board's conservative curriculum standards. It recently adopted social studies changes that Muslim advocacy groups criticized as discriminatory. Read on . 

 
 
