 5. 🤖 Faster, more dangerous cyber war 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Refrigerator hacks. $6.3 billion crypto heists . 14-country espionage campaigns carried out in just 11 days. All in the span of a week.

 Why it matters: State-backed hackers are finding new ways to launch cyberattacks at remarkable speed and scale — and AI threatens to give attackers an even greater edge over defenders, Axios' Sam Sabin reports. 🥑 Driving the news: Hackers tampered with refrigeration systems at dozens of European grocery stores in one night earlier this week — raising temperatures and spoiling food in a sweeping attack some linked to Russia.

 French blood banks were also affected in the attacks on European refrigeration systems, authorities said Wednesday. The Pentagon has also been investigating a series of suspected hacks of refrigeration systems on its military bases over the summer. Nextgov reported that investigators suspect Iran was behind those attacks. 🔬 Zoom in: No clear evidence has yet linked the attack on food supply to AI or automation, but hackers have used those tools in other breaches to speed up operations.

 North Korean hackers behind last week's record-setting Binance heist also likely used automation to move stolen cryptocurrency and cover their tracks, according to Chainalysis. China-linked hackers used an AI-powered tool called Artex to target more than 200 organizations across 14 countries in just 11 days last month, CrowdStrike found . Share this story . 

 
 
