 6. 🔋 Sayonara, Cybertruck 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Gary Hershorn/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Tesla's futuristic Cybertruck got off to a rocky start when its supposedly unbreakable windows shattered during a 2019 demonstration. Seven years later, the polarizing pickup appears headed for the scrap heap.

 Why it matters: The stainless-steel, sharp-edged behemoth attracted plenty of attention, but not enough buyers, Axios transportation correspondent Joann Muller writes. 🛻 Driving the news: Elon Musk says Tesla will wind down Cybertruck production next year to make room for its autonomous Cybercab.

 Musk once envisioned selling at least 250,000 Cybertrucks annually. Sales peaked below 40,000 in 2024 and have been falling ever since. Tesla recalled the Cybertruck 11 times, including twice to reattach stainless steel trim pieces that could fly off while driving.

 Neither the truck's $39,900 price target nor its promised 500-mile driving range ever materialized. Most buyers paid closer to $100,000. Keep reading . 

 
 
